================================================================================================================================
    NGC5291N    needs M_bar =  8.34e8 Msun instead of the measured 15.60 +- 1.7e8 (0.53x, i.e. 0.27 dex), or an external field far stronger than the host can supply
    NGC5291S    needs M_bar =  7.06e8 Msun instead of the measured 15.90 +- 2.1e8 (0.44x, i.e. 0.35 dex), or an external field far stronger than the host can supply
    NGC5291SW   needs M_bar =  2.56e8 Msun instead of the measured  7.10 +- 1.2e8 (0.36x, i.e. 0.44 dex), or an external field far stronger than the host can supply
    NGC7252E    needs M_bar =  1.37e8 Msun instead of the measured  3.90 +- 0.5e8 (0.35x, i.e. 0.46 dex), or an external field far stronger than the host can supply
    NGC7252NW   needs M_bar =  2.19e8 Msun instead of the measured  8.20 +- 0.8e8 (0.27x, i.e. 0.57 dex), or an external field far stronger than the host can supply
    VCC2062     needs M_bar =  0.50e8 Msun instead of the measured  1.40 +- 0.3e8 (0.36x, i.e. 0.44 dex), or an external field far stronger than the host can supply
  [PASS] 46d the size of the gap, stated plainly: to put these six TDGs on the framework's law their baryonic masses would have to be 2-5x SMALLER than measured -- and their baryon budgets are HI-dominated, directly measured from resolved 21-cm maps, and would have to be wrong by more than the total molecular plus stellar content to close it   (required M_bar/measured M_bar = 0.27-0.53 (median 0.36); the measured M_bar errors are 14% on average)

================================================================================================================================
4. the same thing said as a BTFR position -- which is how the hunt list posed it
================================================================================================================================
  The hunt list's promotion criterion for item 46 was 'all TDGs within 0.15 dex of the line'.  The BTFR line the framework
  predicts is M_bar = V_circ^4/(G a_0), with NO external-field correction -- the EFE version is quoted beside it.
  canonical  offset from the bare BTFR line M_bar = V^4/(G a_0): +0.67 to +1.72 dex in M_bar (median +1.29) -- the TDGs are TOO BARYON-RICH for their rotation.  With the external field folded in (canonical solve): median +0.44 dex
  alt        offset from the bare BTFR line M_bar = V^4/(G a_0): +0.76 to +1.80 dex in M_bar (median +1.37) -- the TDGs are TOO BARYON-RICH for their rotation.  With the external field folded in (canonical solve): median +0.44 dex
  [PASS] 46c the hunt list's own promotion criterion is missed, by a lot and in the direction LambdaCDM predicts: the TDGs sit far to the low-velocity side of the framework's BTFR, i.e. they are too baryon-rich for their rotation.  Not one of the six is within the 0.15 dex the item asked for   (canonical: median offset +1.29 dex in M_bar from the bare line (0/6 within 0.15 dex), +0.44 dex once the external field is folded in)

================================================================================================================================
5. the honest defences, computed rather than asserted
================================================================================================================================
  (a) EQUILIBRIUM.  Lelli+2015's own strongest caveat: these HI discs have completed less than one orbit since the merger
      (t_merg/t_orb = 0.2-0.8), so V_circ may not trace the potential.  If the discs are still expanding, the true
      equilibrium velocity would be HIGHER than measured and the tension would shrink.  This is not a defence the data can
      settle, and it applies equally to the LambdaCDM reading, which is why the paper reports both.
  (b) WHICH KINEMATICS.  Milgrom (2007) and Gentile et al. (2007) found MOND SUCCEEDED on the same three NGC 5291 TDGs,
      using Bournaud et al. 2007's kinematics.  Lelli+2015's 3-D disc models lowered V_rot by ~2x and raised M_gas by
      ~1.5-2x, which is what turned the success into a failure.  So item 46's verdict is a bet on whose HI modelling is
      right, not on the acceleration law -- and this script bets on the newer, resolved, 3-D-modelled analysis.
      NGC5291N    with a B07-era gas budget (M_bar / 1.5-2.0) the framework predicts 44-49 km/s; B07's own V_rot for these three was 50-70 km/s (Lelli+2015 App. A), against 45 km/s now -- the older numbers were consistent, the newer ones are not
      NGC5291S    with a B07-era gas budget (M_bar / 1.5-2.0) the framework predicts 37-42 km/s; B07's own V_rot for these three was 50-70 km/s (Lelli+2015 App. A), against 35 km/s now -- the older numbers were consistent, the newer ones are not
      NGC5291SW   with a B07-era gas budget (M_bar / 1.5-2.0) the framework predicts 32-37 km/s; B07's own V_rot for these three was 50-70 km/s (Lelli+2015 App. A), against 28 km/s now -- the older numbers were consistent, the newer ones are not
  (c) THE EXTERNAL FIELD is already included, with the paper's own g_Ne, and it is what brings the prediction down from
      57 to 41 km/s on average against a measured mean of 27.  A stronger field cannot be invoked: it is set by the host's
      measured K-band luminosity and the projected separation, and the projected separation is a LOWER bound on the true one,
      so the true field is if anything WEAKER and the true prediction HIGHER.  That defence is closed.
      NGC7252E    has NO finite external field that works: even the Newtonian floor 19.3 km/s exceeds the measured 18 km/s (by +0.26 sigma, i.e. not significantly -- these two are the two TDGs the framework misses least)
      NGC7252NW   has NO finite external field that works: even the Newtonian floor 21.4 km/s exceeds the measured 21 km/s (by +0.07 sigma, i.e. not significantly -- these two are the two TDGs the framework misses least)
  [PASS] 46e the external-field escape is CLOSED, quantitatively: saving the framework by strengthening the host field alone would need external fields 9-32x the ones the hosts' measured luminosities supply for the 4 TDGs where a finite field exists at all, and for the other 2 there is no such field at any strength because the prediction bottoms out at the Newtonian floor.  The projected separations used are lower bounds, so the true fields are weaker still and the true predictions higher   (required g_Ne / published g_Ne = 9.5x to 32.3x (median 20.4x) for 4/6; unreachable at any field strength: NGC7252E, NGC7252NW)

================================================================================================================================
5b. the combined significance, taken apart -- AGAINST the liability this script is booking
================================================================================================================================
  The +4.2 sigma 'combined' number above adds six z-scores as if they were six independent experiments.  They are not:
  three of them share NGC 5291's data cube, distance, inclination convention and host mass, two share NGC 7252's.  Both
  weakenings and strengthenings of the liability are computed here, and the weakening is stated first.
    per-host mean tension: NGC5291 +1.73 sigma (N=3), NGC7252 +1.96 sigma (N=2), VCC2062 +1.23 sigma (N=1)
    combining ONE point per host instead of one per TDG: +2.84 sigma, against +4.21 sigma naive.
    a Student t on the three host means alone gives t = +7.66 on 2 d.o.f., i.e. p = 0.017 two-sided = 2.39 sigma -- with only three independent systems the t distribution's tails are fat and the large t buys little
    the coherent error that WOULD erase it: every prediction would have to come down by a factor 1.57 (median of 1.29-1.84).  V_pred scales as sqrt(D) here, so that is a distance error of 2.5x;
    Lelli+2015 bound the distance error on these systems at <12% (Hubble flow at V_sys > 4300 km/s; Virgo for VCC 2062),
    which moves V_pred by only 6%.  Alternatively every inclination would have to be wrong by enough to
    raise the measured velocities 1.57x, i.e. sin(i) too large by that factor -- for NGC 7252E, i = 80 deg, sin i = 0.98,
    which cannot be reduced by more than 2% at all.  Neither coherent escape is available.
  [PASS] 46f AGAINST THE LIABILITY, reported before it is banked: the headline '+4.2 sigma combined' is an over-statement, because the six TDGs are three host systems and their errors are not independent.  Collapsing to one point per host gives +2.84 sigma, and a t-test on three host means alone lands under 3 sigma.  The liability survives on the SIGN and on the size of the offset (a factor 1.6 in velocity, 2.8 in mass), not on the naive combined significance, and this script will not quote that combined number without this line attached   (naive +4.21 sigma on 6 TDGs -> +2.84 sigma on 3 hosts, 2.39 sigma by a t-test on the host means (p = 0.017); per-host means NGC5291 +1.73, NGC7252 +1.96, VCC2062 +1.23; the coherent velocity error needed to erase it is 1.57x = a 2.5x distance error against a published 12% bound)

================================================================================================================================
6. mutation controls
================================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 mutation -- switching the kernel off (nu = 1, plain Newton with the same baryons) must CHANGE the answer, and here it must change it in the direction of agreement, since that is the LambdaCDM expectation being tested   (mean tension +1.72 sigma with the kernel, -0.26 sigma without)
  [PASS] M2 mutation -- a_0 raised 10x must make the over-prediction much worse (V ~ a_0^{1/4} in the deep-MOND limit)   (mean tension +1.72 -> +6.18 sigma at 10 a_0)
  [PASS] M3 mutation -- shuffling which TDG gets which measured velocity must inflate the scatter of the tension, i.e. the systematic over-prediction must not be a fluke of six numbers that would look the same in any order   (shuffled scatter 1.14 vs real 0.44 sigma per TDG)

================================================================================================================================
VERDICT
================================================================================================================================
  ITEM 46 -- a LIABILITY, and the sharpest one in this pass, because the two paradigms disagree about the sign and the data
  come down on the other side.
  All six of Lelli et al. 2015's bona-fide tidal dwarfs rotate more slowly than the framework requires.  With the external
  field included and no free parameter, the canonical footing over-predicts by +1.72 sigma per galaxy (+4.21 sigma if the six are
  treated as independent, +2.84 sigma collapsed to their three independent host systems, which is the number to quote;
  chi^2 = 18.9 on 6 points); the alt footing by +1.89 sigma.  A dark-matter-free Newtonian prediction using the same
  measured baryons fits them at -0.26 sigma per galaxy -- which is exactly what LambdaCDM says a tidal dwarf should do.
  On the hunt list's own criterion -- all TDGs within 0.15 dex of the BTFR -- the score is 0 of 6, median offset +1.29 dex.
  Two things are reported in the framework's favour and neither rescues it:
    * the repository's a_0 footings are LOWER than the 1.30e-10 the paper used, which cuts the published tension from
      +4.96 sigma to +4.21 sigma combined.  Smaller, still there.
    * the older kinematics (Bournaud+2007), on which Milgrom 2007 and Gentile+2007 declared a MOND success for the same
      three NGC 5291 objects, WOULD have agreed.  Item 46's verdict therefore rides on trusting Lelli+2015's 3-D HI disc
      models over the earlier position-velocity estimates.  This script trusts the newer analysis and says so.
  The two escapes that could be closed here are closed: the external field is already included at the hosts' own measured
  luminosities and would have to be 20x stronger to work on the four TDGs where any finite field works at all
  (for NGC7252E and NGC7252NW no field of any strength reaches the measurement, because the prediction
  bottoms out at the Newtonian floor), and the baryon budgets would have to be 2.8x too high when they
  are HI-dominated and directly mapped.  The one escape that stays open is dynamical equilibrium: these discs have completed
  less than one orbit since the merger, and Lelli+2015 say so themselves.  That caveat is the reason this is booked as a
  LIABILITY on the ledger rather than as a kill.
  Code note: section 1 reproduces the paper's own published MOND velocities to better than 2% with the Route A kernel, which
  validates the QUMOND external-field implementation shared with h43/h44/h45.

